          #TerminologySingularities.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong (terminology: singularities vs. “global orbits”)
              %br/
              The
              = succeed "-category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2,1)
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CategoryOfOrbiSingularities"}
              is sometimes called the
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+orbit+category"} global orbit category
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functors"} faithful functors
              %em neither
              of these two actually is a “category of orbits” – instead, orbit categories are full subcategories of their slices, by Prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GOrbitsAreReflectiveInSliceOverGOrbiSingularity"}
              below. On the other hand, application of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+equivariant+homotopy+theory"} global equivariant homotopy theory
              to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifolds"} orbifolds
              identifies the category in Def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CategoryOfOrbiSingularities"}
              with the category of archetypical local models for
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbi-singularities"} orbi-singularities
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CategoryOfOrbiSingularities"}
